## Summary

**Root cause:** Every test follows the same pattern — *call the method, maybe `Assert.IsNotNull`, move on*. This gives high line coverage because every production line executes, but **zero behavioral verification**. 6 of 10 tests have no assert at all, and the 4 that do only check `IsNotNull` (which is trivially true for value types and list returns).

This is the classic "coverage-touching" anti-pattern: the tests are a dead-code-detector, not a correctness suite.
